module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.IsRegularSystemOfParameters
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Regular.RegularSequence
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Quotient.Basic
import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalRing.Module
import Sheaf.RingTheory.RegularLocalQuotientKernelParameters
import Sheaf.RingTheory.RegularLocalGeneratorsRegularSequence

/-!
# Lifting the equations of a regular closed fibre

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07NR, parameter-quotient construction.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-regular-fiber-kernel-parameter-lift)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

namespace RingHom

/-- Remove the kernel parameters of a regular closed fibre, retaining regularity of
the base parameters on the quotient.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07NR, proof, paragraphs 1 and 2;
Algebra, Tags 00NR and 00NQ.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-regular-fiber-kernel-parameter-lift)

Proof: Write B = S/m_R S. The map v descends to a surjection B → E because it
annihilates m_R S. Both B and E are regular local, so Tag 00NR gives kernel
generators xbar forming an initial segment of a parameter system of B. Lift
these generators to S. They already map to zero in E: every representative of
xbar does, since m_R S is annihilated by v. Let J be the ideal of these lifts.
The preimage of the kernel in B is J + m_R S, hence ker v = J + m_R S.
The given parameter extension makes the cotangent classes of the base parameters
independent over the residue field of S. The quotient S → B induces an
isomorphism of residue fields, and m_R S is generated by the base parameter
images, so its image in m_S/m_S² is exactly their residue-field span.
The lifted xbar classes are independent modulo their span, because
B has cotangent space m_S/(m_S² + m_R S). Thus the combined classes are independent.
Extend them to a cotangent basis and lift the remaining vectors. Nakayama and
regularity of S say that the resulting list is a regular system of parameters.
Put the lifted kernel parameters first. Tag 00NQ makes S/J regular local and
makes the remaining list, beginning with the base parameters, a regular sequence.
Its initial segment is regular as well. Finally J is proper since J is contained
in ker v, and E is nonzero. If either parameter list is empty the same argument
uses the empty independent family and the zero ideal. -/
theorem exists_regular_fiber_kernel_parameter_quotient
    (R S : Type u) (E : Type v) [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [CommRing E]
    [IsRegularLocalRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing S] [IsRegularLocalRing E]
    (u : R →+* S) (v : S →+* E) [IsLocalHom u] [IsLocalHom v]
    (hv : Function.Surjective v)
    (hkill : (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R).map u ≤ RingHom.ker v)
    (hparams : ∃ (t : List R) (z : List S),
      IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters t ∧
      IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters (t.map u ++ z))
    (hfiber : IsRegularLocalRing (S ⧸ (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R).map u)) :
    ∃ J : Ideal S, J ≠ ⊤ ∧
      RingHom.ker v = J ⊔ (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R).map u ∧
      ∃ t : List R, IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters t ∧
        RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular (S ⧸ J)
          (t.map ((Ideal.Quotient.mk J).comp u)) := by sorry

end RingHom
